        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
It was thought until recently that artificial gravity (AG) could be implemented only by using large radius rotating spacecraft. The investigators have further challenged the assumption that 4-6 rpm is a natural barrier beyond which adaptation becomes unattainable. Fast-spinning small rotational devices are a practical alternative. An integrated set of studies carried out at three universities treats the practical issue of adapting subjects to AG rapidly and with minimal motion sickness (MS) or motor disturbance. 
<p>
Artificial gravity especially when combined with exercise is an attractive antidote to some of the deleterious effects of long-duration space flight. Since practical constraints forbid large radius spinning spacecraft or tethers, emphasis has shifted to short-radius centrifuges (SRC). This entails intermittent exposure to high rotation rates and the likelihood of motion sickness when out-of-plane head movements are made. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for the study isn’t available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Investigators have shown that after a period of adaptation while rotating at 23 rpm, rapid head movements are tolerable for at least 75% of normal subjects. Most of the adaptation can be accomplished within two or three exposures of 20-30 minutes while rotating, spread out over three days. Tilt illusions and the vestibulo-ocular reflex following head turns during rotation depend upon the final orientation of the head relative to gravity. The visual surround during adaptation is relatively unrelated to MS and illusory motion, and only the visual ocular reflex cross axis adaptation seems to require exposure in the light. Arm movements similarly adapt and show little or no long-term after-effects. Anti-motion sickness medication (promethazine) does not seem to interfere with the adaptation process, although it was not effective in reducing MS during high speed CCS. The adaptation is general enough to carry across to a new environment, and even to rotation in the opposite direction, but may not transfer across planes of semicircular canal stimulation. Incremental adaptation, by gradually increasing the rotation speed, is effective but has not been extended all the way up to 23 rpm.
<p>
        </div>

